RESOLVED.CONF(5)                 resolved.conf                 RESOLVED.CONF(5)

NAME
       resolved.conf, resolved.conf.d - Network Name Resolution configuration
       files

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/systemd/resolved.conf

       /etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/*.conf

DESCRIPTION
       These configuration files control local network name resolution.
       ConnMan and other network services (e.g. Tailscale) pass per-link DNS
       servers to systemd-resolved, and /etc/resolv.conf points to its local
       stub resolver on 127.0.0.53.

       Files in /storage/.config/resolved.conf.d/ ending in .conf override the
       defaults in /etc/systemd/resolved.conf. Restart the service to apply
       changes:

           systemctl restart systemd-resolved

       Nameservers in /storage/.config/resolv.conf are added as global DNS=
       servers at boot.

       Use "resolvectl status" to show the current DNS configuration.

OPTIONS
       The following options are configured in the [Resolve] section:

       DNS=
           A space-separated list of IPv4 and IPv6 addresses to use as system
           DNS servers. These are combined with the per-link DNS servers
           provided by ConnMan.

       FallbackDNS=
           A space-separated list of IPv4 and IPv6 addresses to use as the
           fallback DNS servers when no other DNS servers are known. Defaults
           to 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4.

       Domains=
           A space-separated list of search domains. Domains prefixed with
           "~" are routing-only domains.

       MulticastDNS=, LLMNR=
           Takes a boolean or "resolve". Both default to "no" because Avahi
           provides mDNS.

       DNSStubListener=
           Takes a boolean or "udp" or "tcp". Defaults to "yes". Do not
           disable the stub listener; /etc/resolv.conf uses it.

EXAMPLE
       /storage/.config/resolved.conf.d/dns.conf:

           [Resolve]
           DNS=192.168.1.1 1.1.1.1

SEE ALSO
       https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/resolved.conf.html
